{"foreground":"#000000"}}]}}],"style":{},"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}},{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"4z1gg34538","nodes":[],"style":{},"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}}]}],"bulletedListData":{"indentation":0}},{"type":"HEADING","id":"spok130839","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Conclusion: Why Virat Kohli Edges Ahead","decorations":[{"type":"BOLD","fontWeightValue":700},{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"foreground":"#000000"}}]}}],"style":{},"headingData":{"level":2,"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}},{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"sdf5d34668","nodes":[],"style":{},"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}},{"type":"PARAGRAPH","id":"t4mzy30841","nodes":[{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"While Sachin Tendulkar is cricket’s timeless icon, ","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"foreground":"#000000"}}]}},{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":"Virat Kohli’s adaptability across formats, superior averages, and dominance in high-pressure chases","decorations":[{"type":"BOLD","fontWeightValue":700},{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"foreground":"#000000"}}]}},{"type":"TEXT","id":"","nodes":[],"textData":{"text":" make him the better all-round performer in the modern era. His fitness, strike rates, and ability to thrive in T20s—a format Sachin missed—further tilt the scales.","decorations":[{"type":"COLOR","colorData":{"foreground":"#000000"}}]}}],"style":{},"paragraphData":{"textStyle":{"textAlignment":"AUTO"}}}],"documentStyle":{"headerTwo":{"decorations":[{"type":"BOLD","fontWeightValue":700}],"nodeStyle":{}}}},"contentText":"The debate between Sachin Tendulkar and Virat Kohli as the \"greatest Indian cricketer\" has divided fans for years. Both players have redefined cricket in their eras, but their styles, achievements, and impacts differ significantly. Let’s break down their careers to understand who holds the edge. 1. ODI Cricket: Master vs Modern Genius Sachin Tendulkar  dominated ODIs with 18,426 runs  and 49 centuries  over 463 matches, setting benchmarks for consistency and longevity. His iconic 200* against South Africa in 2010 remains unmatched for over a decade. Virat Kohli , however, revolutionized the format with 13,848+ runs  at a staggering average of 58.68  and 50 centuries  in just 292 matches. His ability to chase targets (7,039 runs at 68.33 average) and higher strike rate (93.59 vs Sachin’s 86.24) make him a modern-day chase master. Verdict : Kohli’s efficiency and clutch performances give him the edge in ODIs. 2. Test Cricket: Longevity vs Peak Performance Sachin’s 15,921 Test runs  and 51 centuries  over 200 Tests are monumental. His average of 53.79  reflects his adaptability across conditions and eras. Kohli, with 8,848+ runs  and 29 centuries , trails in totals but leads in double centuries (7 vs Sachin’s 6)  and strike rate (55.56). His aggressive captaincy also boosted India’s overseas Test wins. Verdict : Sachin’s longevity reigns, but Kohli’s peak dominance in tougher conditions is notable. 3. T20Is: Kohli’s Undisputed Kingdom Sachin played just 1 T20I  (10 runs), while Kohli is the format’s legend with 4,188 runs , 1 century , and an average of 48.69 . His 137.04 strike rate  and 38 fifties make him T20’s most reliable batter. Verdict : Kohli owns T20Is—a format Sachin barely touched. 4. Fitness and Fielding: A New Benchmark Sachin’s era prioritized skill over fitness, but Kohli transformed cricket with his athleticism. His 90+% fitness levels  and electric fielding set new standards, inspiring younger players. 5. Legacy and Impact Sachin : Carried India’s hopes in the 90s, inspiring a cricket revolution. His 100 international tons remain iconic. Kohli : Modernized batting with aggression, fitness, and leadership. He holds records like fastest to 27,000 international runs  and most ODI centuries (50). Conclusion: Why Virat Kohli Edges Ahead While Sachin Tendulkar is cricket’s timeless icon, Virat Kohli’s adaptability across formats, superior averages, and dominance in high-pressure chases  make him the better all-round performer in the modern era. His fitness, strike rates, and ability to thrive in T20s—a format Sachin missed—further tilt the scales."}